46
00:04:23,078 --> 00:04:30,120
Every day was a happy day, and every night was peaceful.

47
00:04:30,120 --> 00:04:37,000
Wilbur was what farmers call a spring pig, which simply means that he was born

48
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:43,639
in springtime. When he was 5 weeks old, Mr. Arable said he was now big enough to

49
00:04:43,639 --> 00:04:51,320
sell and would have to be sold. Fern broke down and wept, but her father was

50
00:04:51,320 --> 00:04:56,519
firm about it. Wilbur's appetite had increased. He was beginning to eat

51
00:04:56,519 --> 00:04:59,958
scraps of food in addition to milk. Mr.

52
00:04:59,958 --> 00:05:05,399
Arable was not willing to provide for him any longer. He had already sold

53
00:05:05,399 --> 00:05:09,399
Wilbur's 10 brothers and sisters.

54
00:05:09,399 --> 00:05:15,799
He's got to go, Fern, he said. You've had your fun raising a baby pig, but

55
00:05:15,799 --> 00:05:21,159
Wilbur is not a baby any longer, and he has got to be sold. Call up the

56
00:05:21,159 --> 00:05:26,839
Zuckermanman's, suggested Mrs. Arable to Fern. Your uncle Homer sometimes raises

57
00:05:26,839 --> 00:05:32,200
a pig, and if Wilbur goes there to live, you can walk down the road and visit him

58
00:05:32,200 --> 00:05:37,399
as often as you like. "How much money should I ask for him?" Fern wanted to

59
00:05:37,399 --> 00:05:43,799
know. "Well," said her father, "He's a runt, tell your uncle Homer, you've got

60
00:05:43,799 --> 00:05:48,839
a pig you'll sell for $6 and see what he says."

61
00:05:48,839 --> 00:05:54,839
It was soon arranged. Fern phoned and got her aunt Edith. And her aunt Edith

62
00:05:54,839 --> 00:05:59,879
hollered for Uncle Homer. And Uncle Homer came in from the barn and talked

63
00:05:59,879 --> 00:06:05,559
to Fern. When he heard that the price was only $6, he said he would buy the

64
00:06:05,559 --> 00:06:11,078
pig. Next day, Wilbur was taken from his home under the apple tree and went to

65
00:06:11,078 --> 00:06:17,198
